{"title":"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_September_2024)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In the interior of sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/e/d8efab0f68cfe83cd75f01f951920f544da89d49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC, CA, AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"99\" height=\"16\" /> of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />, any points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/1/a9125414ac35593ff666440128944cc0c0516b94.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L,M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />, respectively, are selected. Prove that the area of at least one of the triangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be6f1fd742df6bd9ee83ed1438075fa536bf027b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AML, BKM, CLK$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"155\" height=\"16\" /> is less than or equal to one quarter of the area of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution\">Solution</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the lengths of sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c52a41dcbd739f1d026c5d4f181438b75b76976.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"31\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/6/5/e657259c0995d2c36d46744bb4aa81471e6575ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CA$\" width=\"28\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"30\" height=\"14\" /> be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/7/3372c1cb6d68cf97c2d231acc0b47b95a9ed04cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$c$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"10\" />, respectively. Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/4/fa46e578342421a5eaf435bd75778660462a2217.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BK=d$\" width=\"65\" height=\"12\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/9/8c9dcb5f9f7fd7f3fd68046b5f941391540a4daf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CL=e$\" width=\"59\" height=\"12\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/0/ab020bd0ce64dd2e000264d876f3226028e32c25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM=f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>Now assume for the sake of contradiction that the areas of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/e/5/1e5df63c931b92f0342074138aee914be8e52856.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta AML$\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448e93b44baa103c82c5987b85510702b2a2d4c0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta BKM$\" width=\"65\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/8/be815c4d7b2b9e91442d9472029ad4c8c1e36767.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta CLK$\" width=\"58\" height=\"13\" /> are all at greater than one fourth of that of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/b/6/3b6d5d23a7e91fb9f6c248fb4cd43111165540f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\Delta ABC$\" width=\"57\" height=\"13\" />. Therefore\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/5/745d44f331bb2aa36c6652ea73948a190f96cd0e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\frac{AM\\cdot AL\\sin{\\angle BAC}}{2}&gt;\\frac{AB\\cdot AC\\sin{\\angle BAC}}{8}\\]\" width=\"338\" height=\"37\" />\n</p><p>In other words, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/0/0/000242f953c027a586193e6f52e34f5a36c134f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AM\\cdot AL&gt;\\frac{1}{4}AB\\cdot AC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"178\" height=\"37\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/1/85123b2d263112f350413762ed2d60cdcb2a2d9d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(b-e)&gt;\\frac{bc}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"105\" height=\"38\" />. Similarly, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/d/15d1eb63414a513dbb24ad8cf7dfd84765323c26.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(c-f)&gt;\\frac{ac}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"33\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/6/1/f617c6ff702a9557bd95bd0d4e038f5fc14501a4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e(a-d)&gt;\\frac{ab}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"38\" />. Multiplying these three inequalities together yields\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/2/d52750b9756876024c1232053ea1c3222dccf7e2.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[def(a-d)(b-e)(c-f)&gt;\\frac{a^2b^2c^2}{64}\\]\" width=\"265\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>We also have that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/5/3d5d7c880cf114093c6295fab54662757e90f217.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d(a-d)\\leq \\frac{a^2}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"108\" height=\"39\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/c/b7c3abdb102c7968843ce37dbfcf7cd9928c4cb5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$e(b-e)\\leq \\frac{b^2}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"39\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/1/56124f297fa87d472722c329f8888b6553017396.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(c-f)\\leq \\frac{c^2}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"107\" height=\"39\" /> from the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Arithmetic_Mean-Geometric_Mean_Inequality\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality\">Arithmetic Mean-Geometric Mean Inequality</a>. Multiplying these three inequalities together yields\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/2/3/7233b18026cb23f531cc92f4d23138cab669b8e6.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[def(a-d)(b-e)(c-f)\\leq\\frac{a^2b^2c^2}{64}\\]\" width=\"265\" height=\"39\" />\n</p><p>This is a contradiction, which shows that our assumption must have been false in the first place. This proves the desired result.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/c/85c5bd921c12760643303f5599299b18ddc896bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AR : AB = x, BP : BC = y, CQ : CA = z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"333\" height=\"16\" />. Then it is clear that the ratio of areas of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/e/ccef96e27f81a4a2f06dfc91664c2b1339e5be3d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AQR, BPR, CPQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"143\" height=\"16\" /> to that of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> equals <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/7/137e1ccfe79395c35d8a03e232a0998dad5e5915.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(1-y), y(1-z), z(1-x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"209\" height=\"18\" />, respectively. Suppose all three quantities exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c339afa03e060d959e4942be7522c0c992f4c41c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"38\" />. Then their product also exceeds <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0cd80ac7637453abf3dbce5fb684c4a53314bbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{64}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" />. However, it is clear by AM-GM that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/f/d3f3b0431a785b6a43be55d2f0f17a5dc1142b4b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(1-x) \\le \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"102\" height=\"37\" />, and so the product of all three quantities cannot exceed <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/c/b0cd80ac7637453abf3dbce5fb684c4a53314bbb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{64}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"20\" height=\"37\" /> (by the associative property of multiplication), a contradiction. Hence, at least one area is less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/2/8/b28bc2bbfff4f6889214058b579ed77fbd9f5308.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4} [ABC]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"64\" height=\"37\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Remarks_.28added_by_pf02.2C_September_2024.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Remarks_(added_by_pf02,_September_2024)\">Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Remarks (added by pf02, September 2024)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Solution 2 is written in a very sloppy way.  However, an interested\nreader can make sense of it.  More importantly, the two solutions\nare identical.  If it wasn't for the sloppy writing, Solution 2 could\nbe obtained from the first Solution after applying a word by word\ntranslation which replaces line segments by ratios.\n</p><p>Below I will give another solution.  It is formally different from\nthe previous solutions, even if not at a deep level.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/3/8c3a2d2224f7d163b46d702132425d47828bf538.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"61\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/9/c79449f0367260f6b3e355679e6db42a933da095.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L, M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" /> be as in the problem.\nDenote <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/8/74809136767dff78c77f0e62b5d9319ec37c5b55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = \\frac{AM}{AB}, y = \\frac{BK}{BC}, z = \\frac{CL}{CA}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"225\" height=\"39\" />\nas in Solution 2.  Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/0/7608e334e1038916c70c7caa92ddf293d8e1dae9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y, z, \\in (0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" /> because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/9/c79449f0367260f6b3e355679e6db42a933da095.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$K, L, M$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"63\" height=\"16\" />\nare in the interior of the respective sides.\n</p><p><span typeof=\"mw:File\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=File:Prob_1966_6.png\" class=\"mw-file-description\"><img src=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/images/b/b7/Prob_1966_6.png\" decoding=\"async\" width=\"400\" height=\"248\" class=\"mw-file-element\" /></a></span>\n</p><p>Using the fact that the\narea of a triangle is half of the product  of two sides and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/f/b/6fb300dacfcde1b57ad070409262b1743e0cd966.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\sin$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"24\" height=\"14\" /> of\nthe angle between them (like in the first Solution), we have that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/0/e4018b8d804d967b11ac58e5051a782918584b9b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{area} AML = x(1 - z) \\mathbf{area} ABC, \\mathbf{area} BKM = y(1 - x) \\mathbf{area} ABC, \\mathbf{area} CLK = z(1 - y) \\mathbf{area} ABC$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"783\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p><p>Now the problem has nothing to do with geometry anymore: we just have\nto show that given three numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/0/7608e334e1038916c70c7caa92ddf293d8e1dae9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x, y, z, \\in (0, 1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"113\" height=\"20\" />, at least one\nof <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/3/cc3dbc5d4a6242f8dba9e7d9721e8e4f00492b3c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(1 - z), y(1 - x), z(1 - y)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"213\" height=\"20\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/4/0/7409a8bbd216ebb3effd09a33ba5ac191ca70a44.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\le \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"34\" height=\"38\" />.\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/d/9/ed90f056058ed044cb1f9a20ff9ceaaccb291f89.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y(1 - x) \\le \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"105\" height=\"38\" />, we are done.  Otherwise, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/0/c/10c071c6450effa29ded046417c87b673e21aa6d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y(1 - x) &gt; \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"105\" height=\"38\" />.  It follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/2/c/62cea45c9bc394bd76eb69170240e3bce01154bb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y &gt; \\frac{1}{4(1 - x)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"105\" height=\"43\" />\n(recall that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/5/7/857bdaa69eb206bcc020506ed06e44ae64b76c5a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0 &lt; x, y, z &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"111\" height=\"16\" />).  In particular, it follows that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/4/4c43d85090b53aa26c0d9061157c35b17f7f9a01.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1}{4(1 - x)} &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"104\" height=\"43\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/5/035c837d16aedc3b7195b1505c26314fb21827f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 - 4x &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/8/6/d867417938f6bacd7430f8790c441fbbb877d70f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z(1 - y) \\le \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" />, we are done.  Otherwise, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/e/5ee5f072c293b84e172c70aa4fc1d813c702e18b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z(1 - y) &gt; \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" />.  Using the inequality on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/2/092e364e1d9d19ad5fffb0b46ef4cc7f2da02c1c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"11\" height=\"13\" /> from the\nprevious paragraph, we have\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/e/20e8cfe4c938a4d47b36963e3ce6a2cc42e6c352.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z \\left( 1 - \\frac{1}{4(1 - x)} \\right) &gt; \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"178\" height=\"45\" />, or after\na few computations, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/2/6b2870c514e8f0a4a8b0b13a3b7cec71f7c46b49.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z \\cdot \\frac{3 - 4x}{1 - x} &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"112\" height=\"40\" />.  Using the\nobservation about <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/0/9703d45b0ba0f9ec065f66ce7e90207ff3b1b86e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 - 4x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"15\" /> from the preceding paragraph, we get\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/0/8f0f1859f286658051febfe1ec43b1b953d154d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z &gt; \\frac{1 - x}{3 - 4x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -15px\" width=\"90\" height=\"40\" />.\n</p><p>Now consider <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/4/e243e5aa010403439e1c0b7ebd78d13c6982f18d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(1 - z)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"20\" />.  Using the inequality on <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/3/b13f21416d84e13708696f34dea81026cda583c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$z$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> from the previous\nparagraph, we have that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/9/909d8e97ee9c5b3d436f339bdac4879c22cd4165.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x(1 - z) &lt; x \\left( 1 - \\frac{1 - x}{3 - 4x} \\right)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"217\" height=\"45\" />.  To finish the\nsolution to the problem, it is enough to show that\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/8/a786c6930890231c86243fe5f09b0b7382330bd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\cdot \\left( 1 - \\frac{1 - x}{3 - 4x} \\right) \\le \\frac{1}{4}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -18px\" width=\"175\" height=\"45\" />.\n</p><p>After some easy computations (and using again that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/3/5/035c837d16aedc3b7195b1505c26314fb21827f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3 - 4x &gt; 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"86\" height=\"15\" />), this\nbecomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/f/6/7f6bfed756cbc87dcd9e15be8fee7def940ca9fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3(4x^2 - 4x + 1) \\ge 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"158\" height=\"21\" />, which is true because.\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/0/4c08159ec6e533d447519ec868bb77fe1980c6f5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$4x^2 - 4x + 1 = (2x - 1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"198\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p><p>[Solution by pf02, September 2024]\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"clear:both; margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO\" title=\"1966 IMO\">1966 IMO</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems\">Problems</a></b>) • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/community/c3222\">Resources</a>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">Problem 5</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"34%\" style=\"text-align:center;\"><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_1\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 1\">1</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 2\">2</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_3\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 3\">3</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_4\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 4\">4</a> <b>•</b> <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1966_IMO_Problems/Problem_5\" title=\"1966 IMO Problems/Problem 5\">5</a> <b>•</b> <a class=\"mw-selflink selflink\">6</a>\n</td>\n<td width=\"33%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Followed&#160;by<br /><b>Last Problem</b>\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=IMO_Problems_and_Solutions\" title=\